00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:08.220 align:start position:0%
in 2012 the SEIU launched an all-out  effort to unionize the fast-food  industry including some 800,000  McDonald's workers it's been three years  and there's been lots of organizing but  the effort is still very far from  winning union recognition anywhere let's  look at the largest chain of hamburger  fast-food restaurants McDonald's  McDonald's uses a franchise structure  meaning that of its 800,000 employees  only around 90,000 technically worked  for the McDonald's Corporation under  current law the other 700 10,000 are  employed by one of the 3,100 McDonald's  franchise owners in the US for the SEIU  to organized the majority of McDonald's  workers the old-fashioned way it would  have to go franchise to franchise  overcoming opposition and negotiating  separate contracts with each franchisee  a process too arduous and inefficient to  make sense  SEIU has successfully gotten around  these kinds of who's the boss issues in  the past in both the janitorial and  security industries essentially the  Union pressured the lead company to  allow unionization of a company that  works for them but reaching a similar  agreement with McDonald's is much more  difficult legally McDonald's can't just  command all of its franchisees to  negotiate with a union however it could  put language in its future franchise  agreements that require the franchises  to negotiate with unions if the union's  can show the employees want union  representation this would make it much  easier to win bargaining with lots of  franchisees who could then choose to  negotiate as a group  McDonald's corporation could also help  the franchisees cover the higher costs  of unionized workers or it could agree  to be treated as a joint employer along  with the franchisees that could be  enough to bring the union the franchisee  is and the corporation all to the  bargaining table but what makes this  unlikely as something simple McDonald's  just doesn't want a union telling it  you
 

